title: Rethinking the Recycling Symbol
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

The original recycling symbol was designed by Gary Anderson in 1970. Its three arrows stand for three steps:

# Separating and collecting recyclable materials
# Processing and manufacturing them into new products
# Purchasing and using recycled materials

Over time it became four or more symbols with two different meanings. These are the four main ones.

!! Product is recyclable

The [[Mobius Band]] arrows on their own. The product or material //can// be recycled. It may not contain any recycled material. Both versions of this symbol mean the same thing.

!! Product is made using recycled materials

The Mobius triangle inside a circle. The product is made from recycled waste, but that doesn't mean it can be recycled again. With no number listed, it means 100% recycled. With a number, like "60%" or "60% post-consumer recycled content", that share is recycled and the rest (40%) is virgin material.

!! Product is made using recycled and non-recycled materials

The same triangle inside an outlined circle, for products with both. The paper industry uses these symbols most, but they carry the same meaning wherever they appear.

!! Does this make sense?

It's a design problem. Graphic design is about clear communication, and these symbols don't communicate clearly.

Open question: how can we //re-solve// it? (your answer)

